# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: sedit: a command to save independently-edited forms to a new file
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: feature
#+DEPENDS: sedit-bugs-and-design (DONE), source-file module (DONE, 1.9.112)

* RESOLVED (2026-09-12, clautolisp 2.2.13 / alfe 2.2.21)

Shipped. The sedit command =w PATH= (word =write=, alias =write-new-file=;
`S' was not usable — sedit downcases tokens, so it collides with `s'=save)
writes the session's file-less top-level forms to a NEW file and registers
their source positions. Design sub-questions decided (pjb): SCOPE = all
file-less forms of the session (a :file session has none — use `s'); EXISTING
PATH = append + merge (the forms are appended, then every form in the resulting
file is re-read so its position is (re)recorded); KEY = =w= (since `S' is
infeasible). Implementation: =clautolisp.sedit::%do-write-new= +
=*sedit-write-new-file-hook*= (modes.lisp), the command in interactor.lisp, and
the hook impl =clautolisp.source-file::write-new-source-file= installed from
source-file.lisp (same pattern as the `s' save hook). Tests:
=write-new-source-file-creates-file-and-registers-positions= /
=…-appends-and-merges-into-existing= (builtins-core suite) and
=sedit-write-new-forwards-file-less-forms-to-the-hook= /
=…-is-a-noop-on-a-file-backed-session= (sedit suite). Both suites green. Closed.

* Context

Split out of =sedit-bugs-and-design.issue= (now closed). That issue shipped
the =clautolisp.source-file= module (1.9.112) and routed sedit's own file save
through it (1.9.113/1.9.114), so forms that already come *from* a file keep a
consistent =*source-position-table*= across edits. It deferred one
forward-looking piece to this issue, quoting its own text:

  "the following API would allow sedit to write forms that have been edited or
   entered independently from file, to new files, and thus having their
   source-position set for further file editing. This would require a specific
   sedit command that will be specified in a separate issue."

* Problem

A form edited or entered in sedit that did NOT originate from a file has no
entry in =*source-position-table*=. It can be evaluated into the image, but it
is not file-associated, so:

  - sedit cannot later edit it *as a file form* (no position to anchor to), and
  - the debugger's rule for offering file-editing — "call clal-sedit with the
    file option only when the form carries source-position information" — never
    fires for it.

There is today no sedit command to take such a form (or a selection of them)
and write it to a *new* file while registering its source-position.

* Proposed command

Add a sedit command (key + M-x name) that writes the selected form(s) to a
chosen new file and registers their source-positions, reusing the existing
=clautolisp.source-file= API (=source-file-open= on a fresh path,
=source-file-insert-toplevel-form= per form, =source-file-save-and-close=) so
the position table is populated exactly as it is for forms loaded from a file.
After the command, the written forms are file-associated and further sedit file
edits behave identically to forms originally loaded from that file.

Open sub-questions for the design pass (pjb to confirm):
  - Scope of "selection": the current form only, a marked set, or the whole
    sedit buffer of independently-entered forms?
  - Behaviour when the target path already exists: refuse, append (merging into
    its position table), or overwrite?
  - The command's binding/name (sedit already uses =s= for "save the current
    file"; this new-file save needs its own key — e.g. =S= / =C-w n= — plus an
    M-x name).

* Acceptance

  - A sedit command writes independently-entered form(s) to a new file.
  - After it runs, each written form has a =*source-position-table*= entry (so
    =(clal-sedit 'file …)= / the debugger's file-edit path works on it), proven
    by a headless unit test that enters a form, runs the command to a temp file,
    reopens it, and asserts the form is found by source position.
  - Existing source-file / sedit tests stay green.

* Notes

Headless-Lisp-doable: the source-file module and sedit session are exercised
without a live ncurses UI in the builtins-core / sedit test suites; the new
command's behaviour (write + position registration) is unit-testable the same
way. The interactive key binding is wired the way the other sedit commands are.
